WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.580
Have you heard about VidMe? Let's talk about VidMe.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.380
So, last week I wrote a tweet thread mocking VidMe, a new competitor to YouTube...

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.940
for their poor site design, hinging on the fact that they don't look serious.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.100
Their site layout makes it look empty, and they are apparently so devoid of content that...

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.560
"Why I'm leaving VidMe" videos are rising to the top.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.760
It's not a good look.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.440
Now, the thread was seen by Alex Benzer, one of the co-founders of VidMe,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:45.500
and Guy Gunaratne, one of the co-founders of Storygami, the platform that powers Maven Video.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.000
Both of them were understandably a bit hurt by the roasting and implored me with a similar plea:

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.320
"Get involved, give feedback, shape the future".

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.340
And that is...

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.560
It's a nice sentiment, but...

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.160
no, I'm not going to do that.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.340
Well, I sort of am, but not directly.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.740
What I'm going to do instead is, later on, I'll rant at length about YouTube's failings.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.580
and let you extrapolate from there

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.160
Online video hosting is expensive, really expensive

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:25.000
An online presence consists of two major cost factors: storage and bandwidth

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.980
and video hosting is kind of the worst of all worlds

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:35.260
With video, there's the added fact that your product, your content, consists of a lot of really large files

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.980
Not only do you require the kind of high bandwidth connection of any popular site

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.620
You also need a lot of storage to host all of that content

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:52.540
Because of this cost reality, it's simple to say that anyone attempting to build a competitor to Youtube is in some pretty deep money

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.760
Anyone who even tries with less will be bankrupt by the end of their first month

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.900
With that in mind,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.360
You're not paying me to do product development,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.100
but I know you can afford it

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.780
You're presumably already paying people to do research and development for you

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.060
And that likely includes people whose job is to search Twitter, Reddit and comment sections

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.740
looking for what people have to say about your product

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.520
Are they talking about it as a positive, as a negative?

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.560
What are they responding to? What's driving them away?

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:31.500
I mean VidMe already has a front page meta category for videos about VidMe

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.110
so

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:38.640
Research and development are already part of your business plan or at least they should be

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.200
and I'm not going to do that for free

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.120
Second the relationship between creators and platforms is inherently hostile

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:54.320
There's a huge incentive in the Internet age to approach everything as just pals

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.840
"We're all on the same side, we're in this together, get involved, help the site grow!"

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.140
The main problem there is that it's not actually true. We're not on the same side

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:11.140
Now, I as a content producer, and you as a platform developer have mutually beneficial goals

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.200
that are pointed in the same general direction

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:22.680
but our incentive sets on the granular level are quite different and diverge as the site's popularity grows

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.600
As the site gets more and more traffic your incentive as a platform

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.880
shifts from managing individuals to managing the product as a whole

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:36.760
and pivots hard towards managing relationships with advertisers instead of content creators

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.560
Of particular importance is that as these interests diverge

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.440
the individual creators lose bargaining power

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.540
RAY KROC: Mack, I'm the president and CEO

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.980
of a major corporation with land holdings in 17 States.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.340
You're on a burger stand on the desert

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.520
I'm national. You're fucking local

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.340
To call back to a previous point

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:05.440
This is why I'm deeply suspicious of the "just pals, help the site grow" mentality.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:06.940
We're just pals

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.320
until we're not

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:13.140
Any platform that doesn't codify their relationship with content creators, that

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:20.260
positions the relationship as "just pals" and "all on the same team" without any structural commitment

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.980
to that relationship is ultimately lying

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.720
It's easy to say that we're all just pals now

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.460
while the platform is new, while its hungry for content to drive traffic, while it's especially hungry for evangelists

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:37.740
that will do a lot of free marketing, purely by virtue of using the platform

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.500
Keeping in mind that a great many super successful YouTubers effectively

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:49.080
don't have a website or online presence outside of the one that exists on other platforms

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.800
Their YouTube Channel is functionally their website

00:04:53.060 --> 00:05:00.200
Failing to codify this relationship ensure that the site will inevitably go through the change in incentives which will in turn lead

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:07.300
to the decision making that overwhelmingly favors advertisers at the direct expense of content creators

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:13.060
Any platform that isn't actively courting already successful content creators

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.500
by offering features and services that those users are looking for is

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.320
mildly doomed

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.940
YouTube is very successful

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.440
Monolithic, even.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.400
If you simply try to recreate YouTube

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.540
who are your users going to be?

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:36.000
Well, generally speaking, it's going to be a lot of users with small to tiny channels,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.500
and it's going to be the people that were too toxic for YouTube

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:49.640
That second one is a far bigger deal than I think most people in the position of platform development really appreciate

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:54.220
If you compete with a monolith the first people to jump on board

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.360
will be the people who were tossed off the other ship

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.200
and most of them were tossed off for a reason

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.680
Now, this can go either way

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:09.920
There's a lot of creators out there who are disillusioned with YouTube's systems, with the culture on the site,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.220
the notoriously awful commenters and the inter channel targeting and harassment

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.560
They haven't necessarily been kicked off

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.200
But many of them have been driven away

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:26.200
The key point is that you get the audience you build, and if you don't build it

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.700
odds are you're going to end up with the dregs

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:35.380
This is why Dailymotion is largely inhabited by automated corporate accounts and

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.740
clickbait pseudoscience peddling diet pills

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.740
If a concerted active effort isn't made to cultivate the types of users that you want to see

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.960
then before you know what's happened, toxic users will have become your core users

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.240
And then you're never going to get out of that hole

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:58.500
So when I go on VidMe and the front page has a substantial concentration

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:04.840
of homophobic punditry and drama channels, it doesn't make me eager to come back

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.980
It tells me that you don't know who you want,

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:13.540
you don't know what YouTubers want, and so you're going to end up with whoever you happen to get

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:19.980
The critical point here is that it shows you don't know what YouTubers actually want

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:25.480
Both of the platforms mentioned, Maven Video and VidMe, are focused on monetization

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:29.110
which, yes, is an important concern of content creators

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:34.260
But there's already a lot of existing viable paths for monetization

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:40.960
While it's perhaps the most emotionally pressing issue, it's not the problem that needs the most solving

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.620
When you elevate monetization solutions to the top of the pile

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:49.500
When that's the selling feature that makes you different from YouTube

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:55.580
It tells me that what you're selling is the image of profitability to a crowd that is otherwise

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.000
not profitable on YouTube

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.080
While there is a certain degree of commendability in the goal of trying to help creators at the lowest tiers

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.400
get more attention and see some financial reward for their work

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:12.360
I'm inherently cynical towards any platform that positions itself in that way

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:19.780
without substantial formalized infrastructure to back it up because, frankly, we've seen it before

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:24.220
And it's because of all your support that we're now announcing this big thing

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.760
that will bring us closer than ever before, by creating a new way for us to create content together

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.940
In early 2016, The Fine Brothers attempted to launch React World

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.780
a brand licensing platform on a similar sort of promise

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.420
Using their platform would be a great opportunity to get paid for making reaction videos

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.440
because you would get the exposure that comes from using the React World brand

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:55.120
But they had no control infrastructure to back it up, there was no quality control, and thus no guarantee of performance.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.000
It was pure rent-seeking

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:02.440
Maker Studios in early 2017 went through a massive upset that was years in the making

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:08.700
They recruited tens of thousands of small channels with a promise of better ad rates, high quality brand deals

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:13.720
and the vague promise of exposure and promotion with no solid commitments

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.800
In reality they delivered on none of those

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:23.620
and simply skimmed ad revenue off the top of the 60,000 channels they had brought into their network

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:28.000
It finally came to a head when Pewdiepie's racist practical jokes

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.740
made it clear that no one was at the wheel,

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.740
that Maker wasn't in the business of content and talent development

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:42.540
and were in fact just more rent seekers, luring in creators with poorly backed promises of getting paid

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.909
And they stole money from a lot of people

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.360
So if you're a new video platform and your core selling point your big launch feature is a tip jar

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.720
Forgive me if I'm having some Deja vu but I'm pretty sure I've heard this song before

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.620
I want to stress that point, the launch feature aspect

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.480
We live in a world after YouTube

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:13.240
YouTube already exists, YouTube already has over a decade of development

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:18.000
misdevelopment, feature creep, and general design cruft

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:22.140
YouTube has a number of vestigial features that are good ideas

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:25.820
but either they don't work well or they're functionally abandoned

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:28.900
because they're just tacked on or poorly implemented

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.900
Any new platform try to compete doesn't really need to reinvent the wheel

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:38.740
They're not figuring this out for the first time they just need to figure out how to make certain features work,

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:43.640
how to improve them, how to fold them into the core structure of their service

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.660
It's the launch features of a platform that are going to tell you the most

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:52.760
about a platform's values both in design and business

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:58.140
What features did they consider to be core to the identity and structure of the platform?

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.360
Where are their priorities?

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:05.760
There's a lot of successful YouTubers who would gladly jump ship from YouTube to another platform

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.840
if that platform offered features that on YouTube are either lacking or just plain bad

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:18.120
See, what YouTubers actually want is a platform that will take security seriously

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.720
that will care about harassment, abuse,

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:27.180
malicious use of automated services, will care about filtering and moderation tools and collaboration tools

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:32.200
YouTube has a feature that lets you cluster videos together as a packaged show

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:39.360
But it sucks, and no one uses it and even if you are using it the user experience is bad and lame

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:45.100
Contrast this with how the now-defunct Blip TV structured itself around serial content

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:51.900
Blip was far from perfect, but the user experience for watching something as a show was pretty good

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:58.480
It created a sense of continuity that's largely absent, or at best a tertiary consideration on YouTube

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:04.260
Despite the fact that YouTube is now the underpinning platform for some full-size businesses

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:10.400
YouTube's structure is still awful at handling any sort of multi-user setup,

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:16.720
let alone properly transitioning from a personal channel to a professional one managed by a team

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:22.560
If your channel is attached to a personal Google account, including say, your email

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:29.660
the only way to give someone access to the back-end of the channel is to give them access to the entire account

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.780
Additionally, powers cannot be partitioned between team members

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:41.420
Anyone with access to the channel enjoys full access to upload, download, delete, rename, moderate everything

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:49.000
This creates significant security concerns as compromising any individual member of the team compromises everything

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.360
YouTube's Playlist system is functional but hardly elegant

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:58.280
and the overall structure of the site as a whole makes it troublesome for content creators

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:03.280
to effectively compartmentalize content without segmenting their viewership

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:11.140
A secondary channel for specific types of content is effectively starting from scratch and needs to be maintained as its own entity

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.960
Additionally there's no way for channel managers to effectively migrate content from one channel to another

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:21.680
without going through the process of reuploading from scratch, requiring that all secondary data

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:27.700
from names to descriptions to captions and subtitles and thumbnails be re-entered, manually

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:33.360
Oh, and you lose existing comments, views, votes, and so on in the move

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:38.180
These are just a few of these structural site design issues with YouTube

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:41.920
not even addressing the cultural problems with a lack of moderation

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:47.649
unclear and unevenly enforced community standards and easily exploited automated systems

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:55.900
There are a wealth of deficiencies with YouTube that could all be centered as a core selling point for a competitor

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:58.300
To bring this back around I mentioned this

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:02.940
but it's my belief that you already employ people to do research and development

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.900
and did some market research before you even started building your platform

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:11.320
To me that seems like just a sensible business decision

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:12.640
You need to know your competitors,

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.080
you need to know your users, and you need to know the market

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.640
If you don't have that, well...

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.460
it doesn't exactly fill me with confidence

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:26.220
If you do have that and this is the site that you chose to launch

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:32.480
These are the features that you considered vital, that you figured would capture the audience that you're looking for

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.580
Well, it doesn't exactly fill me with confidence

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.220
Put simply, if someone's roasting your site on Twitter

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:45.920
your co-founder shouldn't be imploring them on Twitter to submit feedback and make the site better

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.200
because you should already know

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:50.100
If you haven't done your research,

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:58.520
if you haven't worked systems for security, moderation, and collaboration into the core infrastructure of the site

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:03.660
if those features aren't foundational to your design, then it's already too late

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.420
It means that you haven't built a YouTube competitor

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:11.900
You've just built a YouTube clone, and you're dooming yourself to the same mistakes

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:17.680
A frankenstein product built of cobbled-together features that don't properly integrate

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:24.920
The charitable reading is that you're just unprepared which isn't a good look on its own, but the worse reading

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:31.860
is that you're grifters, rent seekers, looking to exploit the hopes and labor of content creators

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.860
and the internet is already full of those

